The '''BMT Broadway Line''' is a [[rapid transit]] line of the [[B Division (New York City Subway)|B Division]] of the [[New York City Subway]] in [[Manhattan]]. {{As of|2016|November}}, it is served by four services, all colored {{NYCS const|color|yellow}}: the {{NYCS trains|Broadway express}} on the express tracks and the {{NYCS trains|Broadway center local weekday}} on the local tracks during weekdays (the N and Q trains make local stops during late nights, as do the N and R trains on weekends). The line is often referred to as the "N and R",<ref>{{cite news|first=Randy|last=Kennedy|newspaper=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2000/10/31/nyregion/honoring-the-champions-tunnel-visions-trapped-in-subway-missing-the-parade.html|title=Honoring the Champions|date=October 31, 2000|page=B10|issn=0362-4331|quote=And the only reason she was standing in the middle of Broadway was that she was below it. Underground. In the N and R subway station.}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|first=Susan|last=Saulny|newspaper=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2000/12/05/nyregion/in-subway-changes-w-follows-v-but-for-riders-it-s-not-so-simple.html|title=In Subway Changes, W Follows V, but for Riders It's Not So Simple|date=December 5, 2000|access-date=May 31, 2019|page=B1|issn=0362-4331|quote=The Q, N, R and W trains would all run on N and R tracks in Manhattan.|archive-date=May 31, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190531200309/https://www.nytimes.com/2000/12/05/nyregion/in-subway-changes-w-follows-v-but-for-riders-it-s-not-so-simple.html|url-status=live}}</ref> since those were the only services on the line from 1988 to 2001, when the [[Manhattan Bridge]]'s southern tracks were closed for rebuilding. The Broadway Line was built to give the [[Brooklyn Rapid Transit Company]] (later the [[Brooklyn–Manhattan Transit Corporation]], or BMT) access to [[Midtown Manhattan]].

The line is named for its location under [[Broadway (Manhattan)|Broadway]] between [[Vesey Street]] and [[Seventh Avenue (Manhattan)|Seventh Avenue]]/45th Street ([[Times Square]]). It also passes under Vesey Street, [[Whitehall Street]], [[Trinity Place]], and [[Church Street (Manhattan)|Church Street]] in [[Lower Manhattan]], and Seventh Avenue, [[59th Street (Manhattan)|59th Street]], and [[60th Street (Manhattan)|60th Street]] in [[Midtown, Manhattan|Midtown]]. The local tracks stretch the entire length between the two [[East River]] tunnels: the [[Montague Street Tunnel]] to the [[BMT Fourth Avenue Line]] in [[Brooklyn]] and the [[60th Street Tunnel]] to the [[BMT Astoria Line]] and [[60th Street Tunnel Connection]] in [[Queens]]. Center express tracks exist between [[Canal Street (BMT Broadway Line)|Canal Street]] and [[57th Street–Seventh Avenue (BMT Broadway Line)|57th Street]], turning off at Canal Street to feed the [[south tracks on the Manhattan Bridge]], and continuing north and east under [[Central Park]] as the [[63rd Street Lines|IND/BMT 63rd Street Line]] (connecting with the [[Second Avenue Subway]]). The Broadway Line was the only Manhattan outlet north of [[Delancey Street]] for the BMT's Brooklyn lines until 1967, when the opening of the [[Chrystie Street Connection]] allowed most [[BMT Brighton Line]] and [[BMT West End Line]] service to be moved to the [[IND Sixth Avenue Line]].

